sweet-cntvct-checkpoints (boot-speed, docs/research/45-fast-boot-claims-and-xnu-gap.md s3 items 3/4): CNTVCT stamps at ten early-boot
points, so the ~34 s between shim jump and the first kernel_bootstrap lines can be split on the next run. Boot-arg sweetcntvct=1 prints
them; DEFAULT (no boot-arg) = the stamps are stored (one register read each, no I/O) and nothing is printed, so behaviour is unchanged.
Stamps (index: place): 0 arm_init entry (after mark 1); 1 after PE_init_platform(FALSE) (mark 2; boot-args readable from here);
2/3 before/after arm_vm_init; 4 after serial_init; 5 after "kprintf initialized" (mark 20); 6/7 before/after the first printf in
kernel_bootstrap (startup.c); 8/9 before/after pmap_bootstrap (arm_vm_init.c, inside arm_vm_init). Dump: ck0-5 by kprintf right
after the first kprintf (arm_init.c), then ck0-9 again by printf after kernel_bootstrap's first printf (reaches the console ring and
the USB replay). Each line: "SWEET CNTVCT ckN=<raw ticks> hz=<timebase_frequency_hz>"; host converts ticks/hz.
Facts, read from source (xnu-8796.141.3 with the stack applied): clock = ml_get_hwclock() (osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c:2082, ISB +
CNTVCT_EL0 read :2070-2077, declared arm/machine_routines.h:758), the same clock as patch 0115 (sweetlogts), so the stamps line up with
the [SSSSS.mmm] prefixes. hz = 0 before pe_identify_machine (0115 header): the raw ticks are the datum. Boot-args are NULL until
PE_init_platform (0115 header; the dump checks PE_state.bootArgs). kprintf is declared pexpert/pexpert.h:233 (a no-op if
CONFIG_NO_KPRINTF_STRINGS, :241-244: then only the printf dump appears). The stamp array is a static in sweet_early.c (SWEET only;
no-op inlines in sweet_early.h otherwise). Not verified on hardware. Shim-side stamps (research 45 s3 items 1-2) are a separate shim
change and not in this patch.
